"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" numbers will occur exactly once. And this is a solution."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Also, we can find a primitive root "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and $g^{0}, g^{1}, g^{n-3}, g^{3}, g^{n-5}, \\cdots } is also a solution."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Proof:"}],"kind":"strong"},{"kind":"text","text":" "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For a composite number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 4 it can either be written as the products of two numbers "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 1."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≠ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then we immediately get "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pq"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"|("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)!."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", note that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 4 so 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"|("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"|("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)! always holds which means "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/8d9dc2fca825c236ba6aa5200c6fab143d87fccd45043ac0fba69f07d504be88.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/487/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"487D - Conveyor Belts"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This problem can be solved by classic data structures."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For example, let's try something like SQRT-decomposition. Let's divide the map horizontally into some blocks. For each grid, calculate its destination when going out the current block (or infinite loop before going out current block)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each modification, recalculate the affected block by brute force. For each query, we can just use the \"destination when going out the current block\" to speed up simulation."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be the size of a block, then the time for each modification is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), for each query is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nm"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), since at most "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nm"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") blocks, and at most 1 grid of each block are visited."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The total time complexity is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nm"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"qnm"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pS"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the number of modifications. Let "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f47137b091718eca247a8b70177943fc71a94353b0c2e97f812bd649fa22b345.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", the complexity can be the best: "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0e54716ccb6fc4c98a0e40919d15748056d1cef59bfa0e319e2200162c15391c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This task can also be solve by segment tree. The time complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0201c35c276d44a2a65ca2cbd419b1d2095b4451fc7116f0ba37cd982e288241.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", or "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/6924c67b1094aee7be5b246f86dcea8b59dfd4cb588c2c9034cadd60a21d8c6b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", depending on implementation."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/487/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"487E - Tourists"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First we can find out all cut vertices and biconnected components(BCC) by Tarjan’s Algorithm. And it must form a tree."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"From the lemma below, we know that if we can pass by a BCC, then we can always pass any point in the BCC."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We use a priority queue for each BCC to maintain the minimal price in the component."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each modification, if the vertex is a cut vertex, then modify itself and its related BCCs’ priority queue. If not, modify the priority queue of its BCC."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each query, the answer is the minimal price on the path from x (or its BCC) to y (or its BCC). We can use Link-Cut Trees or Heavy-Light Decomposition with Segment Trees."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To be more exact, we can only modify the father BCC of the cut vertex in order to guarantee complexity(otherwise it would be hacked by a star graph).When querying, if the LCA of x and y is a BCC. Then the father of the LCA(which is a cut vertex related to the BCC) should also be taken into account."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The time complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/8f748036e3c5cd94d97b10d03a4de2b275aeb37e6e6d7949c3e77dd8a77e3b72.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" or "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/cc7932901db8c529790e71008441d31358e141cd28a1f5bb5217f31ff5688fff.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Lemma:"}],"kind":"strong"},{"kind":"text","text":" In a biconnected graph with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ 3 points, for any three different vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", there is a simple path to from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" going through "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Proof:"}],"kind":"strong"},{"kind":"text","text":" Consider a biconnected graph with at least 3 vertices. If we remove any vertex or any edge, the graph is still connected."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We build a network on the graph. Let's use (u,v,w) to describe a directed edge from u to v with capacity w. For each edge (u,v) of the original graph, we build (u,v,1) and (v,u,1). Build (S,c,2), (a,T,1) and (b,T,1). For each vertex other than S,T,c, we should give a capacity of 1 to the vertex."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In order to give capacity to vertex u, we build two vertices u1,u2 instead of u. For each (v,u,w), build (v,u1,w). For each (u,v,w), build(u2,v,w). Finally build (u1,u2,1)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Hence, if the maximal flow from S to T is 2, there is a simple path from a to b going through c."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we consider the minimal cut of the network. It is easy to find that minimal cut <= 2, so let's prove minimal cut > 1, which means, no matter which edge of capacity 1 we cut, there is still a path from S to T."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If we cut an edge like (u1,u2,1), it is equivalent to set the capacity of the vertex to 0, and equivalent to remove the vertex from the original graph. The graph is still connected, so there is still a path in the network."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If we cut other edges, it is equivalent to remove an edge from the original graph. It is still connected, too."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we have minimal cut > 1, which means maximal flow = minimal cut = 2. So there is always a simple path from a to b going through c."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/14832"}